<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brendan the Navigator</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 484–c. 577</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Saint Brendan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Brendan the Navigator (c. 484–c. 577) was an Irish monk and saint, born according to tradition near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tralee">Tralee</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-kerry">County Kerry</a>. He founded several monasteries, most famously <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clonfert">Clonfert</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-galway">County Galway</a> around 557. He is best known from the Navigatio Sancti Brendani, a medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> tale of his voyage with fellow monks to a &quot;Promised Land of the Saints&quot; across the Atlantic, which was widely read across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brendan-the-navigator.jpg" alt="Brendan the Navigator" width="310" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown mediaeval scribe. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_brendan_german_manuscript.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
